+module VERIFICATION-NO-BRIDGE
+  imports MPY
+
+  syntax Int ::= decimalDigit(Int, Int) [function, total]
+  rule decimalDigit(N:Int, 1) => pyMod(N, 10)
+  rule decimalDigit(N:Int, P:Int)
+    => pyMod((N -Int pyMod(N, P)) /Int P, 10)
+    requires N >=Int 0 andBool P >Int 1
+
+  syntax Int ::= decimalDigitSum(Int) [function, total]
+  rule decimalDigitSum(N:Int)
+    => decimalDigit(N, 1)
+       +Int decimalDigit(N, 10)
+       +Int decimalDigit(N, 100)
+       +Int decimalDigit(N, 1000)
+       +Int decimalDigit(N, 10000)
+    requires 0 <=Int N andBool N <=Int 10000
+
+  syntax Str ::= binaryNumeral(Int) [function, total]
+  rule binaryNumeral(N:Int) => str(binCodes(N))
+endmodule

codex
The bridge-free audit exposed the expected symbolic limitation: K cannot prove the universal list-slice identity because `buildIS` and symbolic length remain opaque; the attempted universal connection theorem failed with that exact residual, not a parser/tool error. I’m confirming that the main theorem likewise stalls without the bridge and separately checking all 46 slice results reachable from this program under both fixed and bridge-enabled execution.
